OrangeOS: JavaScriptCore's JIT with W^X, through a dual-mapped memfd.

OrangeOS never maps memory writable and executable at once, so the Linux
JIT, which reserves its pool read/write/execute, could not start and JSC
ran on its interpreter. JSC already supports keeping code pages
read/execute and writing them through a second, writable mapping
(ENABLE(SEPARATED_WX_HEAP), used by Darwin through mach_vm_remap). This
adds the Linux form of it: the pool is reserved read/execute, one memfd
is mapped over it read/execute and once more read/write, and
performJITMemcpy writes through the writable alias. The build turns it
on with -DENABLE_SEPARATED_WX_HEAP=1 (tools/wpe/build_deps.py).

- ExecutableAllocator.cpp: initializeSeparatedWXHeaps for Linux; if the
  aliases cannot be made, the JIT is left off rather than writable.
- ExecutableAllocator.h: the pool is not reserved writable.
- X86Assembler.h: code patches go through performJITMemcpy (as with
  MPROTECT_RX_TO_RWX) instead of storing into code directly.

userland/bin/jit-alias-probe checks the kernel side (docs/design/012).

